00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:42.610
We're not there yet.
我们还没到那一步呢。

876
00:31:42.770 --> 00:31:44.090
You know, this is a stranger.
要知道，对方是个陌生人。

877
00:31:44.510 --> 00:31:44.830
Yeah.
没错。

878
00:31:44.830 --> 00:31:46.770
You don't drink your beverage in an interview.
面试的时候不能喝饮料。

879
00:31:47.050 --> 00:31:47.190
Okay.
好的。

880
00:31:47.490 --> 00:31:47.750
Okay.
好的。

881
00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:51.490
Second, remember, you are interviewing them too.
第二，记住，你也在面试他们。

882
00:31:51.930 --> 00:31:53.690
An interview is like a first date.
面试就像第一次约会。

883
00:31:53.770 --> 00:31:56.750
You're there to discern whether or not this is a match.
你是去判断双方是否合适。

884
00:31:56.850 --> 00:31:59.330
And it has to be a match for both people.
而且这对双方来说都必须是合适的。

885
00:31:59.410 --> 00:32:00.530
It has to go both ways.
必须是双向的选择。

886
00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:06.230
And that always brought me some comfort because it made me kind of like less needy because
这总能给我带来一些安慰，因为它让我没那么卑微，因为

887
00:32:06.230 --> 00:32:12.110
I was remembering like, hey, I'm here to discern if I like this place too.
我记得，嘿，我也是来判断自己是否喜欢这个地方的。

888
00:32:12.310 --> 00:32:12.490
Right.
没错。

889
00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:13.710
I have a say in this.
在这件事上我有发言权。

890
00:32:13.810 --> 00:32:14.630
I have some worth.
我有我的价值。

891
00:32:14.830 --> 00:32:17.270
And I'm going to take agency for myself.
我要为自己做主。

892
00:32:18.070 --> 00:32:18.490
Okay.
好的。

893
00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:24.630
Third, I think it's always good to be ready with some sort of icebreaker to get some banter going.
第三，我觉得准备一些破冰话题来开启闲聊总是好的。

894
00:32:24.770 --> 00:32:27.430
You don't want to launch into your skills right away.
你不想一上来就直接推销自己的技能。

895
00:32:27.830 --> 00:32:31.010
So what is something that could spark a connection?
那么，有什么话题能引发共鸣呢？

896
00:32:31.770 --> 00:32:35.810
Sometimes I would go in the rooms and I would kind of look around and kind of clock things.
有时我走进房间，会环顾四周，留意一些东西。

897
00:32:36.250 --> 00:32:39.510
Like, oh, where do these people, do they have a vacation photo up?
比如，哦，这些人，他们有没有挂度假照片？

898
00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:41.130
Can that spark a conversation?
那能开启一段对话吗？

899
00:32:41.590 --> 00:32:46.090
One time I walked in and this woman had this beautiful blue ring on.
有一次我走进去，看到一位女士戴着一枚漂亮的蓝色戒指。

900
00:32:46.190 --> 00:32:48.550
And I said, oh, my goodness, I love your ring.
我说，天哪，我太喜欢你的戒指了。

901
00:32:48.770 --> 00:32:50.990
And she said, oh, it's an aquamarine.
她说，噢，那是海蓝宝石。

902
00:32:51.050 --> 00:32:52.650
I'm a March baby.
我是三月出生的。

903
00:32:52.810 --> 00:32:54.130
And I said, I'm a March baby.
我说，我也是三月出生的。

904
00:32:54.290 --> 00:32:57.390
And then we had a little bonding moment about both being Pisces.
然后我们因为都是双鱼座，产生了一点共鸣。

905
00:32:57.390 --> 00:32:59.670
I think these little things are very helpful.
我觉得这些小细节非常有帮助。

906
00:32:59.910 --> 00:33:04.870
Because remember, businesses are interested in your skills and your abilities.
因为你要记住，企业不仅看重你的技能和能力。

907
00:33:05.050 --> 00:33:08.750
But they are also going to have to, like, be around you on a personal level.
他们还得在个人层面上和你相处。

908
00:33:08.990 --> 00:33:09.530
So, right?
所以，对吧？

909
00:33:09.610 --> 00:33:11.250
This rapport, it's helpful.
这种融洽的关系很有帮助。

910
00:33:11.530 --> 00:33:19.170
You know, Greg Daniels once told me that people want to work with people that they think they can spend a 12-hour day with.
你知道，格雷格·丹尼尔斯曾告诉我，人们想和那些他们觉得能一起共事12小时的人一起工作。

911
00:33:19.530 --> 00:33:20.010
Yeah.
没错。

912
00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:21.530
And I've always thought about that.
我一直把这句话记在心里。

913
00:33:22.370 --> 00:33:25.430
That was a little bit of, I guess, interview advice I took to heart.
我想，这算是我铭记在心的一条面试建议。

914
00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:34.450
And then my mom said when I would go in for a big meeting, a big interview, she would say, you just go in there and you be politely bored.
我妈曾说，每当我参加重要会议或面试时，她都会告诉我，你就进去，表现得礼貌而淡定。

915
00:33:35.210 --> 00:33:36.010
Politely bored.
礼貌而淡定。

916
00:33:36.210 --> 00:33:36.510
Mm-hmm.
嗯。

917
00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:39.030
Like, they need to get you interested.
意思是，他们得想办法让你感兴趣。

918
00:33:39.330 --> 00:33:39.610
Mm-hmm.
嗯。

919
00:33:39.690 --> 00:33:41.290
You're not there to get them interested.
你不是去求着让他们对你感兴趣的。

920
00:33:42.250 --> 00:33:44.990
Oh, that's another way to go, isn't it?
噢，这又是另一种思路，不是吗？

921
00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:48.030
But you can still have an icebreaker.
但你依然可以准备一个破冰话题。

922
00:33:48.430 --> 00:33:48.850
Yeah.
没错。

923
00:33:49.110 --> 00:33:53.370
But, you know, it's that thing that you said where you're taking assessment of them as well.
不过，就像你说的，这也是一个你同时在评估对方的过程。

924
00:33:53.650 --> 00:33:53.970
Yes.
是的。

925
00:33:54.650 --> 00:33:55.130
Exactly.
没错。

926
00:33:55.550 --> 00:33:56.190
All right.
好的。

927
00:33:56.270 --> 00:34:03.050
My next bit of advice is you know that you're going to have to talk about why you're a good fit for the job.
我的下一条建议是，你要知道你肯定会被问到为什么你适合这份工作。

928
00:34:03.350 --> 00:34:03.490
Yeah.
没错。

929
00:34:03.610 --> 00:34:06.550
Of all the things they might ask you, we know this is happening.
在他们可能问的所有问题中，我们确定这题必考。

930
00:34:06.550 --> 00:34:08.270
So be prepared.
所以要做好准备。

931
00:34:08.490 --> 00:34:12.190
Have a self-pitch about yourself that you can say in three sentences.
准备一段三句话以内的自我推销词。

932
00:34:12.830 --> 00:34:19.750
Be sure that it's specific to the job description, to the company, that it's not just general.
确保内容针对职位描述和公司情况，不要泛泛而谈。

933
00:34:19.950 --> 00:34:20.910
Do your homework.
做好功课。

934
00:34:21.190 --> 00:34:21.490
Mm-hmm.
嗯。

935
00:34:21.790 --> 00:34:29.370
And finally, especially if you're interviewing for the first time or if you haven't interviewed in a while, practice with someone before you go.
最后，特别是如果你是第一次面试，或者很久没面试了，去之前一定要找人练习一下。

936
00:34:29.610 --> 00:34:30.810
I did this.
我就这么做过。

937
00:34:31.110 --> 00:34:34.990
I had been going on some general meetings and nothing was happening.
当时我参加了一些常规会议，但都没什么下文。

938
00:34:34.990 --> 00:34:38.750
And my manager said, maybe we should practice the meetings.
我的经纪人说，也许我们该演练一下这些会议。

939
00:34:38.990 --> 00:34:40.170
So we did a practice session.
于是我们进行了一次模拟练习。

940
00:34:40.350 --> 00:34:44.990
And in our practice session, she asked me, what kind of acting are you interested in doing, Jenna?
在练习中，她问我：珍娜，你对哪种类型的表演感兴趣？

941
00:34:45.290 --> 00:34:47.690
And I gave the answer that I always gave.
我给出了我一贯的回答。

942
00:34:47.690 --> 00:34:50.450
I would say, well, you know, I love comedy.
我说，嗯，你知道的，我喜欢喜剧。

943
00:34:50.450 --> 00:34:51.450
I love drama.
我喜欢正剧。

944
00:34:51.790 --> 00:34:56.170
I would be interested in film, television, independent film.
我对电影、电视剧、独立电影都感兴趣。

945
00:34:56.390 --> 00:34:58.170
I wouldn't say no to a soap opera.
肥皂剧我也不会拒绝。

946
00:34:58.410 --> 00:34:59.370
I love theater.
我热爱戏剧。

947
00:34:59.590 --> 00:35:01.070
And she was like, let me stop you right there.
她当时就说，打住。

948
00:35:01.170 --> 00:35:02.330
This is not the right answer.
这个回答不对。

949
00:35:02.510 --> 00:35:03.930
I don't know who you are.
我根本不知道你是谁。

950
00:35:03.930 --> 00:35:05.770
She said, here's your new answer.
她说，这是你的新答案。

951
00:35:06.010 --> 00:35:11.310
Your answer is, I would love to be cast on an ensemble comedy television series.
你的回答是：我非常希望能出演一部群像喜剧电视剧。

952
00:35:11.710 --> 00:35:12.910
Yeah, specific.
没错，要具体。

953
00:35:13.490 --> 00:35:14.010
Specific.
具体。

954
00:35:14.230 --> 00:35:14.350
Yeah.
对。

955
00:35:14.470 --> 00:35:14.970
The end.
结束。

956
00:35:15.170 --> 00:35:15.330
Yeah.
是啊。

957
00:35:15.470 --> 00:35:25.090
Because obviously, you can do all these things, but you need to give people a vision of you specifically and what is your main skill.
因为很明显，你可以做所有这些事情，但你需要给人们一个关于你个人的愿景，以及你最擅长的技能是什么。

958
00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:25.750
Yeah.
没错。

959
00:35:26.010 --> 00:35:27.590
And I started doing that.
我开始这样做之后。

960
00:35:27.710 --> 00:35:30.610
And after I did that, my meetings really started to pay off.
在我这样做之后，我的面试真的开始有成效了。

961
00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:31.370
Yeah.
是啊。

962
00:35:31.610 --> 00:35:32.750
That's my advice.
这就是我的建议。

963
00:35:32.750 --> 00:35:33.570
I love it.
我喜欢这个建议。

964
00:35:33.730 --> 00:35:35.710
I love getting specific with things.
我喜欢把事情具体化。

965
00:35:35.910 --> 00:35:36.570
You know this.
你是知道的。

966
00:35:36.770 --> 00:35:37.190
Yes.
是的。

967
00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:42.250
I mean, whether it's a prayer, a meditation, a goal, get specific.
我的意思是，无论是祈祷、冥想还是目标，都要具体。

968
00:35:42.690 --> 00:35:45.490
My mom always says get specific with the Lord, you know?
我妈妈总是说要向上帝具体地祈求，你知道吗？

969
00:35:45.890 --> 00:35:46.730
But I do.
但我确实是这么做的。

970
00:35:46.870 --> 00:35:49.910
I write out a goal or something I want to pray about.
我会写下一个目标或者我想祈祷的事情。

971
00:35:50.170 --> 00:35:51.650
I write it on a Post-it note.
我把它写在便利贴上。

972
00:35:51.770 --> 00:35:52.910
I put it on my bathroom mirror.
贴在我的浴室镜子上。

973
00:35:53.390 --> 00:35:54.850
I know this about you.
关于你这一点我很了解。

974
00:35:54.950 --> 00:36:00.970
And I can say in my experience personally, the more specific I am, the better results I get.
我可以根据我个人的经验说，我越具体，得到的结果就越好。

975
00:36:01.490 --> 00:36:01.930
Always.
总是这样。

976
00:36:02.290 --> 00:36:02.510
Always.
总是这样。

977
00:36:02.650 --> 00:36:02.890
Always.
总是这样。

978
00:36:03.370 --> 00:36:05.970
So when you're putting it out there, folks, get specific.
所以各位，当你们在表达诉求时，要具体。

979
00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:13.070
I would like to add one thing that I did have a job once where I hired people and I also had to fire people.
我想补充一点，我曾经有一份工作，既要负责招聘，也要负责解雇员工。

980
00:36:13.330 --> 00:36:15.410
Oh, so you've been on the other side of this.
噢，所以你曾经站在过另一边。

981
00:36:15.490 --> 00:36:15.790
Yes.
是的。

982
00:36:15.930 --> 00:36:18.310
What advice would you give someone from that position?
从那个角度来看，你会给别人什么建议？

983
00:36:18.310 --> 00:36:30.150
I would say the minute you arrive, be polite to everyone because you don't know from the person that held the door open for you to maybe someone who helped direct where you parked.
我会说，你一到那里，就要对每个人都礼貌，因为你不知道为你开门的人，或者帮你指引停车位的人是谁。

984
00:36:30.410 --> 00:36:34.610
All of those people that are there matter to who you're interviewing with.
所有在场的人对于你要面试的对象来说都很重要。

985
00:36:35.030 --> 00:36:35.510
Yes.
是的。

986
00:36:35.750 --> 00:36:35.930
Right?
对吧？

987
00:36:36.090 --> 00:36:37.050
That's really good advice.
这建议真的很好。

988
00:36:37.250 --> 00:36:45.550
So be respectful to everyone you encounter, no matter what their position you might think, because even though their position might not dictate whether or not you get the job,
所以要尊重你遇到的每一个人，无论你认为他们的职位如何，因为即使他们的职位可能决定不了你是否能得到这份工作，

989
00:36:45.690 --> 00:36:47.750
they're important to this company.
但他们对这家公司很重要。

990
00:36:48.310 --> 00:36:54.150
And then the other thing I would say is just think about how you smell.
另外我还要说的一点是，注意一下你的气味。

991
00:36:54.370 --> 00:36:54.770
Okay.
好的。

992
00:36:54.830 --> 00:36:55.030
Okay?
明白吗？

993
00:36:55.390 --> 00:36:55.570
Yeah.
好的。

994
00:36:55.570 --> 00:36:58.750
When you go to interview with someone, you're usually in a smaller office.
当你去面试时，通常是在一个小办公室里。

995
00:36:58.930 --> 00:36:59.170
Mm-hmm.
嗯。

996
00:36:59.810 --> 00:37:02.190
And your smell will come in with you.
你的气味会随你一起进入房间。

997
00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:02.930
Okay.
好的。

998
00:37:03.190 --> 00:37:07.970
It'll be the unique thing that walks in the room with you that sets you apart.
那将是随你进入房间的独特之处，让你与众不同。

999
00:37:08.390 --> 00:37:12.010
Try not to overdo it with the cologne or the perfume.
尽量不要喷太浓的古龙水或香水。

1000
00:37:12.370 --> 00:37:12.730
Mm-hmm.
嗯。

1001
00:37:13.270 --> 00:37:17.650
Not saying this is going to rule you out, but there will be a thought that goes through someone's mind.
我不是说这会让你被淘汰，但别人心里难免会犯嘀咕。

1002
00:37:17.750 --> 00:37:22.110
It's kind of like, oh, my gosh, am I going to have to sit downwind of this person?
就像是，天哪，我以后是不是得坐在离这人下风口的地方？

1003
00:37:22.270 --> 00:37:22.370
Yeah.
没错。

1004
00:37:22.510 --> 00:37:24.590
Am I going to have to sit with Drakkar all the time?
我以后是不是得一直跟“德拉卡”古龙水味儿坐在一起？

1005
00:37:24.590 --> 00:37:25.430
Drakkar all day.
整天都要闻着德拉卡味儿。

1006
00:37:25.670 --> 00:37:25.850
Yeah.
是啊。

1007
00:37:26.070 --> 00:37:26.210
Yeah.
没错。

1008
00:37:26.630 --> 00:37:26.750
Keep it light.
别太沉重。

1009
00:37:26.750 --> 00:37:30.030
If your smell precedes you, think about it.
如果你的气味比你先到，那你就得考虑一下了。

1010
00:37:30.030 --> 00:37:31.510
This is very good advice.
这建议非常棒。

1011
00:37:31.710 --> 00:37:31.890
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

1012
00:37:32.030 --> 00:37:32.910
We've nailed it.
我们搞定了。

1013
00:37:33.030 --> 00:37:33.790
I think we have.
我想是的。

1014
00:37:34.410 --> 00:37:36.190
A in Sacramento, good luck.
萨克拉门托的A，祝你好运。

1015
00:37:36.370 --> 00:37:37.170
Go get them.
去拿下他们。

1016
00:37:37.390 --> 00:37:37.910
Yes.
好的。

1017
00:37:38.170 --> 00:37:39.070
We're rooting for you.
我们为你加油。

1018
00:37:39.910 --> 00:37:44.170
Our final question of the day comes from M in Oregon.
今天最后一个问题来自俄勒冈州的M。

1019
00:37:44.870 --> 00:37:48.810
M, I really feel like Angela Martin would have strong opinions about this one.
M，我真的觉得安吉拉·马丁会对这个问题有强烈的看法。

1020
00:37:48.990 --> 00:37:49.210
Okay.
好的。

1021
00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:53.410
And also, M, you are talking to three cat ladies.
而且，M，你现在是在跟三位爱猫女士对话。

1022
00:37:54.050 --> 00:37:55.150
Cassie is a cat lady.
凯西也是位爱猫女士。

1023
00:37:55.270 --> 00:37:56.050
Cassie, are you on?
凯西，你在吗？

1024
00:37:56.410 --> 00:37:57.250
Yes, I'm here.
在，我在这儿。

1025
00:37:57.410 --> 00:37:58.950
I'm going to want you to weigh in on this, too.
我也想让你对此发表一下看法。

1026
00:37:59.590 --> 00:38:00.810
Jenna, here we go.
珍娜，我们要开始了。

1027
00:38:01.750 --> 00:38:10.950
M says, I was flabbergasted when a coworker recently asked me to check up on his cats while he was away for three weeks.
M说，最近有个同事让我帮他照看三周的猫，我当时惊呆了。

1028
00:38:11.430 --> 00:38:15.690
I have coworkers with whom I have friendships, and this wouldn't be a problem with them.
我有关系好的同事，如果是他们，这根本不是问题。

1029
00:38:16.070 --> 00:38:20.890
But this male colleague and I have never done much more than a casual chit-chat.
但这位男同事和我平时除了闲聊几句，基本没交集。

1030
00:38:21.250 --> 00:38:28.930
We've never seen each other outside of work and have never even had lunch together, other than a few team lunches paid for by our boss.
我们从没在工作之外见过面，除了老板请客的几次团队午餐，我们甚至没单独吃过饭。

1031
00:38:29.350 --> 00:38:32.030
It also wouldn't have been a problem if we were neighbors.
如果我们是邻居，这也不会是个问题。

1032
00:38:32.170 --> 00:38:37.050
I would totally swing by for a cat visit, even if I didn't know a neighbor very well.
哪怕邻居不太熟，我也完全愿意顺路去喂喂猫。

1033
00:38:37.390 --> 00:38:40.750
But he lives 25 minutes away from my house.
但他住的地方离我家有25分钟车程。

1034
00:38:40.950 --> 00:38:43.010
This seems like a bold ask to me.
我觉得这要求提得太冒昧了。

1035
00:38:43.330 --> 00:38:46.930
I would never ask a casual work acquaintance such a favor.
我绝不会向一个普通的职场熟人提这种请求。

1036
00:38:47.290 --> 00:38:52.190
I told him that I thought it would make better sense for him to ask someone who lived a bit closer to him.
我告诉他，我觉得他找个住得近点的人会更合适。

1037
00:38:52.370 --> 00:38:53.390
What are your thoughts?
你们怎么看？

1038
00:38:53.390 --> 00:38:58.790
It was such a strange request that I can't let go of it, and my husband is tired of me talking about it.
这个请求太奇怪了，我一直耿耿于怀，我丈夫都听烦了。

1039
00:38:59.270 --> 00:39:00.950
Signed, would-be cat sitter.
署名，准猫咪保姆。

1040
00:39:01.590 --> 00:39:02.510
M in Oregon.
俄勒冈州的M。

1041
00:39:02.870 --> 00:39:08.210
I mean, we know what happened to Angela's cat when she asked Dwight to watch it once.
我是说，我们都知道安吉拉让德怀特帮忙看猫时发生了什么。

1042
00:39:08.210 --> 00:39:08.970
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

1043
00:39:09.590 --> 00:39:10.970
Frozen in her refrigerator.
被冻在冰箱里了。

1044
00:39:11.370 --> 00:39:11.810
Yes.
是的。

1045
00:39:12.070 --> 00:39:12.870
I'm with you.
我支持你。

1046
00:39:12.990 --> 00:39:17.110
I think it's a big ask for someone you don't know that well and who lives far away from you.
我觉得对于一个不熟且住得远的人来说，这确实是个过分的要求。

1047
00:39:17.430 --> 00:39:21.070
Well, I would start by saying I think you handled it well.
嗯，首先我想说，我觉得你处理得很好。

1048
00:39:21.290 --> 00:39:21.730
Yes.
没错。

1049
00:39:22.350 --> 00:39:25.730
I think Em's big question is how do I let this go?
我觉得M最纠结的是该怎么放下这件事？

1050
00:39:26.110 --> 00:39:26.550
Yes.
是的。

1051
00:39:26.550 --> 00:39:28.110
Here was what I thought.
我是这么想的。

1052
00:39:28.510 --> 00:39:33.470
I thought maybe this person just doesn't have anyone to ask.
我想也许是因为这个人没别人可以拜托了。

1053
00:39:33.670 --> 00:39:36.010
Maybe they don't have a lot of friends.
也许他们没什么朋友。

1054
00:39:36.650 --> 00:39:43.390
Maybe this person views you as one of the nicest people in the office.
也许这个人把你视为办公室里最友善的人之一。

1055
00:39:43.610 --> 00:39:46.550
I'm not saying this means that you have to do it.
我并不是说你非得答应不可。

1056
00:39:46.610 --> 00:39:48.470
You do not have to do it.
你完全没必要答应。

1057
00:39:48.770 --> 00:39:50.910
This is a big ask.
这确实是个不小的请求。

1058
00:39:50.910 --> 00:39:51.750
It's too far away.
距离太远了。

1059
00:39:51.890 --> 00:39:52.730
It's not convenient.
很不方便。

1060
00:39:53.350 --> 00:39:58.930
But in terms of just letting it go, maybe there's a way to frame it where you say, oh,
但如果只是想放下这件事，或许你可以换个说法，比如，噢，

1061
00:39:59.170 --> 00:40:02.110
maybe this person just doesn't have anyone else.
也许这个人真的没别人可以找了。

1062
00:40:02.330 --> 00:40:04.250
Well, Jenna, you went very zen on that.
好吧，Jenna，你这番话真是太佛系了。

1063
00:40:04.350 --> 00:40:07.250
And you really are taking the moral compass high ground here.
你确实站在了道德的制高点上。

1064
00:40:07.470 --> 00:40:08.990
And I think you have a good point.
而且我觉得你说得很有道理。

1065
00:40:09.410 --> 00:40:09.930
Thank you.
谢谢。

1066
00:40:10.670 --> 00:40:11.150
Cassie?
Cassie呢？

1067
00:40:11.790 --> 00:40:13.170
Ooh, this one's tough.
噢，这个问题很棘手。

1068
00:40:13.170 --> 00:40:19.170
But yeah, I would say I'm sorry I can't and never say why you can't.
但没错，我会说抱歉我帮不上忙，而且永远不要解释原因。

1069
00:40:19.250 --> 00:40:23.570
I think sometimes we feel like we have to come up with an excuse of like why you can't
我觉得有时候我们总觉得必须找个借口来解释为什么不能

1070
00:40:23.570 --> 00:40:24.150
do something.
做某件事。

1071
00:40:24.490 --> 00:40:24.870
Oh, yeah.
噢，没错。

1072
00:40:25.570 --> 00:40:30.530
But then, yeah, if you want to go above and beyond, maybe you can recommend like a cat
但如果你想做得更周到些，或许可以推荐一个猫咪

1073
00:40:30.530 --> 00:40:36.570
service or if you happen to know someone close by or just kind of, yeah, if you feel like
看护服务，或者如果你刚好认识附近的人，或者，如果你觉得

1074
00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:40.870
they're lonely, you can kind of like brainstorm with them like, oh, maybe there's a neighbor
他们很孤独，你可以和他们一起出出主意，比如，噢，也许有位邻居

1075
00:40:40.870 --> 00:40:44.990
that has like a cat in the window and you could talk to them and see if they can help out.
窗边养了猫，你可以去问问他们能不能帮忙。

1076
00:40:45.130 --> 00:40:49.330
And that would be great because then when that person goes out of town, then they can
那样会很棒，因为当那个人出远门时，他们就可以

1077
00:40:49.330 --> 00:40:55.390
be like cat sitters for each other, which is kind of like how I do it when I'm out of
互相帮忙照顾猫咪，我出远门的时候就是这么做的，

1078
00:40:55.390 --> 00:40:55.530
town.
我找的都是住得离我很近的人。

1079
00:40:55.590 --> 00:40:57.650
I have people that are like nearby me.
这样就不会觉得是个很大的麻烦了。

1080
00:40:57.650 --> 00:40:59.430
So it doesn't feel like such a big ask.
所以我喜欢这个建议。

1081
00:41:00.010 --> 00:41:01.110
Yeah, I like that.
没错，我也喜欢。

1082
00:41:01.270 --> 00:41:05.190
I think there's a way to frame it to be like, first of all, thank you so much for asking
我觉得可以这样表达：首先，非常感谢你邀请

1083
00:41:05.190 --> 00:41:06.230
me to check on your pet.
我来照顾你的宠物。

1084
00:41:06.410 --> 00:41:07.910
I know what your pet means to you.
我知道你的宠物对你意味着什么。

1085
00:41:07.910 --> 00:41:11.870
So thank you for trusting me with this animal that you care so much about.
所以谢谢你如此信任我，把这么重要的宠物托付给我。

1086
00:41:12.150 --> 00:41:16.810
But because of that commute, I recommend having someone closer to your animal.
但因为通勤距离的原因，我建议找个离你宠物更近的人。

1087
00:41:17.830 --> 00:41:23.590
Yeah, but Em is clearly obsessing over why this ask would have been made in the first
没错，但Em显然一直在纠结为什么对方会提出这个请求。

1088
00:41:23.590 --> 00:41:24.090
place.
比如，为什么是我？

1089
00:41:24.590 --> 00:41:25.710
Like, why me?
为什么是现在？

1090
00:41:25.890 --> 00:41:26.690
Why now?
我觉得你就把它当作一种赞美吧。

1091
00:41:26.830 --> 00:41:28.470
I think take it as a compliment.
但不要因为坚持这个界限而感到内疚。

1092
00:41:28.670 --> 00:41:31.250
But do not feel guilty for having this boundary.
你不想为了照顾不太熟的同事的猫而特意绕路开车25分钟，这是完全合理的。

1093
00:41:31.510 --> 00:41:35.590
It's totally appropriate that you do not want to drive 25 minutes out of your way to check
没错。

1094
00:41:35.590 --> 00:41:37.890
on your co-workers' cats that you barely know.
这完全合情合理。

1095
00:41:38.050 --> 00:41:38.570
That's right.
说得对。

1096
00:41:38.690 --> 00:41:39.810
That's totally fair.
这完全公平。

1097
00:41:39.990 --> 00:41:43.010
And I think it's also okay that you were thrown by the requeSt.
而且我觉得你被这个请求弄得不知所措也是正常的。

1098
00:41:43.150 --> 00:41:43.470
Yes.
是的。

1099
00:41:43.690 --> 00:41:44.970
Because I think we would be too.
因为我觉得换做是我们也会一样的。

1100
00:41:45.110 --> 00:41:47.070
I actually think Em did a great job here.
我其实觉得Em处理得很好。

1101
00:41:47.170 --> 00:41:47.370
Yeah.
没错。

1102
00:41:47.470 --> 00:41:48.510
You did great, Em.
你做得很好，艾姆。

1103
00:41:48.610 --> 00:41:50.330
And that's from three cat ladies.
而且这可是出自三位爱猫女士之口。

1104
00:41:51.090 --> 00:41:52.190
There you have it.
就是这样。

1105
00:41:52.330 --> 00:41:53.950
That's our first Dear Office ladies.
这是我们第一期“亲爱的办公室女士”环节。

1106
00:41:54.670 --> 00:41:56.110
Let's talk about this, Angela.
我们来聊聊这个吧，安吉拉。

1107
00:41:56.390 --> 00:41:57.350
How do you feel now?
你现在感觉如何？

1108
00:41:57.350 --> 00:41:58.530
You've given advice.
你已经给出了建议。

1109
00:41:58.810 --> 00:41:59.810
How do you feel?
你感觉怎么样？

1110
00:42:00.110 --> 00:42:02.430
I mean, I really enjoyed hearing from people.
我是说，我真的很喜欢听听大家的想法。

1111
00:42:02.570 --> 00:42:03.550
That's what I love.
这就是我所热爱的。

1112
00:42:03.550 --> 00:42:04.970
I love hearing from people.
我喜欢听大家分享。

1113
00:42:05.170 --> 00:42:06.870
I love problem-solving about life.
我喜欢解决生活中的难题。

1114
00:42:07.170 --> 00:42:10.070
You know, I want to know what's in your sauce packet drawer.
你知道的，我想知道你们的酱料包抽屉里都有些什么。

1115
00:42:10.610 --> 00:42:11.430
Yes, you do.
没错，你确实想知道。

1116
00:42:11.650 --> 00:42:11.850
Yeah.
是啊。

1117
00:42:12.010 --> 00:42:13.430
I enjoyed this as well.
我也很享受这次交流。

1118
00:42:13.490 --> 00:42:15.190
So hopefully you did too.
希望你们也一样。

1119
00:42:15.190 --> 00:42:17.350
And maybe we'll be back for more.
也许我们还会再回来做这个环节。

1120
00:42:17.550 --> 00:42:20.310
And to everyone who sent in their letters, thank you so much.
非常感谢每一位寄来信件的朋友，真的非常感谢。

1121
00:42:20.490 --> 00:42:22.510
Yes, please keep sending in your questions.
是的，请继续给我们发来你们的问题。

1122
00:42:22.650 --> 00:42:25.290
We're going to keep this up on officeladies.com.
我们会把它保留在officeladies.com网站上。

1123
00:42:25.690 --> 00:42:27.690
And Angela, I want to throw something at you.
安吉拉，我想向你提个建议。

1124
00:42:27.910 --> 00:42:28.290
What?
什么？

1125
00:42:28.530 --> 00:42:30.130
I think we got a little confidence here.
我觉得我们现在有点信心了。

1126
00:42:30.350 --> 00:42:31.370
What do you think?
你觉得呢？

1127
00:42:31.370 --> 00:42:34.490
We have people send in any kind of advice question.
我们可以让大家发来任何类型的咨询问题。

1128
00:42:34.570 --> 00:42:36.410
It doesn't just have to be about work.
不一定非得是关于工作的。

1129
00:42:36.510 --> 00:42:36.970
Oh my gosh.
天哪。

1130
00:42:37.010 --> 00:42:37.670
Do you want to try?
你想试试吗？

1131
00:42:37.910 --> 00:42:38.230
Okay.
好啊。

1132
00:42:38.410 --> 00:42:39.390
Yes, we'll try.
好的，我们会试试的。

1133
00:42:39.670 --> 00:42:39.990
All right.
行吧。

1134
00:42:40.150 --> 00:42:43.210
So send us your questions and we can't wait to sort it all out with you.
所以请把你们的问题发给我们，我们迫不及待想和你们一起理清这些困惑了。

1135
00:42:44.310 --> 00:42:46.250
I guess we'll be doing this again.
看来我们还会再做这个环节的。

1136
00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:47.790
So thanks again.
再次感谢大家。

1137
00:42:47.950 --> 00:42:50.710
And thanks to Jordan for our intro music.
也要感谢乔丹为我们制作了片头音乐。

1138
00:42:50.710 --> 00:42:51.630
We loved it.
我们很喜欢。

1139
00:42:52.010 --> 00:42:53.230
We'll see you next week.
下周见。

1140
00:42:53.390 --> 00:42:53.830
See you then.
到时候见。

1141
00:43:01.020 --> 00:43:02.800
Thank you for listening to Office Ladies.
感谢收听《办公室女士》。

1142
00:43:03.100 --> 00:43:07.220
Office Ladies is produced by Earwolf, Jenna Fisher, and Angela Kinsey.
《办公室女士》由Earwolf、珍娜·费舍和安吉拉·金赛制作。

1143
00:43:07.220 --> 00:43:09.600
Our senior producer is Cassie Jerkins.
我们的高级制作人是凯西·杰金斯。

1144
